WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.400
May Day, May Day, May, come in base.

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:05.600
Commander, I'm picking up a faint transmission.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:09.660
Repeat, made, under attack, hideous creatures.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:11.280
Captain, your signal is weak.

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:12.740
Please retransmit, over.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:21.960
We've defeated thousands, sub-alpha missiles, malfunction, stealth seals down, my men, desperate, horrible.

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:23.980
Captain, get a hold of yourself.

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:25.720
It's only a video game.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:26.860
Do you copy? Captain!

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:29.100
Captain, your name is Nostalgia.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:32.120
Galaga, it's only a video game.

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:55.680
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:56.680
I'm Mike.

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:59.140
I'm Sean. And I'm Joe.

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:04.500
And if you didn't pick it up from the beginning there, we are talking about Galaga,

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:10.160
a game that definitely doesn't have stealth shields or any sort of defense mechanisms.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:13.100
Did you guys notice that?

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:14.080
You know what?

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:18.280
I thought maybe I just forgot that there was a button for the stealth shields, but yeah.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:22.160
Yeah. I thought they were just so stealthy that you wouldn't have seen them.

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:23.740
Ooh, yeah. Or noticed them.

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:31.400
And for anyone who's concerned, we're doing this completely remotely, so it will sound a lot different than our usual episodes,

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:36.980
but we're taking the necessary precautions here, making sure that we keep the show alive, right guys?

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:38.200
Don't get that Rona. Absolutely.

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:45.180
All right, so let's just talk about Galaga for a minute because this is one that I'm sure both of you had heard of before, correct?

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:46.220
Oh yeah. Yes.

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:59.260
Played a bunch. So Galaga first came to the arcades way back in 1981, and we've seen this a few times now where arcade games from like the early 80s get ported to the NES,

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:00.640
and this one's no different.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:09.660
The only thing that's weird here is that this game already came to the Famicom way back in 1985, so it's not even that the port took long to make or anything,

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:14.900
it's just that it was sitting on shelves waiting for its opportune North America moment.

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:22.780
And that's just so strange to me because this is such an iconic game that you'd think that it would be within the first year or two.

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:28.040
Right, you would have thought that this would have came in like that first third party wave that happened in 86.

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:37.020
Yeah. Because Namco was kind of already releasing games on the NES at that point, or at least publishing them.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:41.600
So it seems weird to leave out Galaga, but maybe they know something that I don't.

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:47.080
Yeah, and if you're doing what we're doing for some reason and actually going through in order on the NES,

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:54.120
it's a little jarring to jump into this game, which just for better or worse feels like it's from a totally different time period.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:58.340
Yeah, very much. It's you don't really see that too much nowadays.

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:11.880
It seems that most games that come out either that they either abide by the new design principles that have been established over the years or they're they're hearkening back to old ones on purpose.

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:15.040
You don't really just see like, oh, here's a six year old game.

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:17.460
It's getting released for the first time on home consoles.

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:30.860
What I would say is kind of the equivalent is is the the way that now we always see like Final Fantasy 10 HD remaster now for PS4 where it's like, OK, they didn't change anything particularly about the game.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:33.780
They just made it look nicer and released it again.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:40.120
So it's like a lot of people are playing that game for the first time on new systems, but it's an old thing, you know, I hear you.

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:54.400
Yeah. And they also did something strange here, which is and I don't know if this is a genius marketing move or whatever, but they added that subtitle demons of death to the Galaga name, which is yes.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:55.240
That's so ridiculous.

00:03:55.460 --> 00:04:08.640
But at the same time, it's not a bad move because you would think that you're getting like a sequel to Galaga when you're actually just getting the same exact version as the arcade game just with a new subtitle.

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:14.040
I think that this still exists in a way I can't think of any specific examples off the top of my head.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:28.600
But when ports happen, whether they're ports between systems or ports between console generations, they usually give it a subtitle for no reason, at least with like Japanese releases, I feel.

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:38.260
Yeah. And I think it's also important since we're since we're talking about Galaga so much already that we need to mention what Galaga really is in case you're not informed.

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:51.860
And the best way I could think of it is if you've ever played Space Invaders, it's like that, except for the enemies actually like move and have attack patterns that you would see in like shmup games later on.

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:57.340
So I actually, it's interesting you say that you say in shmup games later on.

00:04:57.480 --> 00:04:59.760
I was going to ask you, would this be considered a shmup?

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:01.520
I wouldn't consider it a shmup.

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:05.040
So what sets this, I didn't think so either, but I don't really know.

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:10.400
I can't really put my finger on the actual like parameters that set this aside from a shmup.

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:12.620
I don't know if there's any actual parameters.

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:17.920
I think, well, the one big one is that you don't have full control.

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:20.300
Motion. You can only move left or right.

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:26.980
But I think that's sort of a goofy reason to keep it out of a genre.

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:39.400
Like it's sort of like how genres turn into these sort of like just gatekeeping situations where it's like, oh, that's not that.

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:43.020
But like, wait, it's got all of these features that make it this genre.

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:43.980
But no, that's not that.

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:47.900
I think it is like at its basis form a shmup.

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:53.940
I would say that it's not a shmup because and this is going to sound like gatekeeping.

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:55.260
So just keep me honest here.

00:05:55.420 --> 00:06:00.620
But I think that it's not because there's no navigation of a level.

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:06.800
Right. You're always just fixed down at the bottom of the screen and there's no obstacles to dodge.

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:08.920
Let alone, you know, the enemies can only come at you.

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:11.900
You can't choose to work your way around the enemies.

00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:14.100
There's also no power ups.

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:16.780
I mean, I know that there's one that we'll get into.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:21.080
But for the most part, there's no like upgradable, customizable ship here.

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:31.820
And there's no like there's no thematic thing that takes you from like the big boss at the end of a stage or even at the end of a game.

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:48.060
Sure. And that's sort of that would make me think this is more of like a proto shmup or like all the things that you just described that this game doesn't have were were evolutions within the genre and not something that places it apart from it.

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:49.360
But I don't know.

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:51.360
It's it's totally subjective.

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:53.460
But I agree with what you're saying there.

00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:55.460
Actually, that makes a lot of sense.

00:06:55.600 --> 00:07:03.160
And I do think when I was playing this that I could see a lot of ways that games like Gradius would learn from the success of Galaga.

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:05.780
Yeah. All right.

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:09.080
So let's talk about the actual gameplay here for a minute.

00:07:09.100 --> 00:07:14.160
And that could really be whatever you guys want it to be because the gameplay is so repetitive here.

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:19.360
I would love to even get like initial impressions of the gameplay loop from you guys.

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:20.620
Were you liking it at first?

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:21.640
Was it too difficult?

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:23.080
Did it take some getting used to?

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:24.380
Sean, why don't you start?

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:30.080
So Galaga, it's really hard to separate like.

00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:42.300
It's hard to think of a game that is so iconic in the early days of video games and break it down like when it's this simple.

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:51.560
Like I, I would say that upon playing it for this podcast, I became more critical of it.

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:58.500
I think that it is it becomes difficult to a fault in the later levels.

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:07.360
Maybe that's a holdover from its attempt to be a quarter eater back in the day.

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:14.540
But I don't know if, are you asking like, what is the gameplay or how did I feel about the gameplay?

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:17.560
How did you feel about the gameplay loop?

00:08:18.020 --> 00:08:22.900
Just, you know, from the get go, like you don't have to talk about like your overall stance on the game.

00:08:22.900 --> 00:08:31.180
I guess I meant like upon initial boot, knowing everything you know about the NES now, was this just too disappointing to enjoy?

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:33.000
Or were you, were you able to have fun?

00:08:33.020 --> 00:08:39.580
I mean, I had fun in the fact that it is a very, it's a good emulation of the arcade game.

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:50.180
It's a little disappointing now because we've seen so many leaps within the more current games and what they've been able to do with the hardware.

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:53.580
But, you know, games like this have its place.

00:08:53.800 --> 00:09:04.640
I think that whether it's a shmup or not, having, it being so similar at its core and having played all these other shmups, which I generally don't care for.

00:09:05.660 --> 00:09:18.180
And then playing this, where I know we've talked about before the fact that there are people that just want to, that do enjoy just like honing their skill and just doing something that's like, how many of these can I do without any like bells or whistles?

00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:20.040
I'm not one of those people.

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:28.460
So for me to play this, taking like a shmup and stripping even like power-ups and things away from it, I was pretty bored with it, to be honest.

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:33.980
And I'm a person who doesn't have any nostalgia for this game.

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:38.500
Not nostalgia, not nostalgia for this game.

00:09:38.840 --> 00:09:41.380
And I've heard of it, but I've never played it.

00:09:41.380 --> 00:09:44.920
And I just, it was kind of, for me, it was just like, yeah, I get it.

00:09:45.080 --> 00:09:46.240
I've played things like this.

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:52.020
Which is a little sad to me, too, because I do like going back to old games and old styles and seeing what's interesting about it.

00:09:52.060 --> 00:09:56.400
But this is just hitting that sweet spot of like what I don't generally care for.

00:09:56.760 --> 00:09:58.960
So it wasn't really doing it for me.

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:05.420
I actually liked, I think the basicness of it was working in its favor at least early on for me.

00:10:05.780 --> 00:10:10.700
Because, you know, now that we've seen so many different shmups, I didn't think of this as a shmup at all.

00:10:10.780 --> 00:10:12.200
I thought of it like an arcade game.

00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:18.320
And to figure out like the difference, there's only four different types of enemies as far as I can remember.

00:10:18.580 --> 00:10:22.320
And they all have the same pattern for each of the type of enemy.

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:31.160
So you got, you almost like, you got into this state of flow in the game where you knew like, oh, those are the enemies with wings.

00:10:31.180 --> 00:10:35.400
And they're coming, the, whatever, they look like, they look like bees almost.

00:10:35.620 --> 00:10:36.560
And they come down first.

00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:39.120
And they do this thing where they swoop around behind you.

00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:44.020
And at first, I was always getting killed by them because I wasn't watching them until they left the screen.

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:46.300
But then you kind of learn through it.

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:56.420
And so I think that even if I never got like past like stage 10 of this game, it was still fun to kind of keep playing those early levels again and seeing how my high score would go.

00:10:57.060 --> 00:11:00.800
Oh, so now you are looking at your high score, huh?

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:02.420
I thought you would say that.

00:11:02.420 --> 00:11:16.760
So my defense there is that it's similar to playing the same levels in Balloon Fight over and over again, in that it's just fun in the sense of going over and doing the same thing but trying to improve.

00:11:17.320 --> 00:11:22.160
Whereas like in a game like Castlevania, I wouldn't say it's fun to just keep playing the first level.

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:23.940
That's not that you would want to keep going.

00:11:24.260 --> 00:11:25.140
I understand.

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:27.820
I just wanted to yank your chain, Mike.

00:11:28.720 --> 00:11:29.600
I understand.

00:11:31.820 --> 00:11:36.880
And so there's a couple of like – there's a couple of small details in this game though, right?

00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:46.900
Besides the fact that the enemies have different patterns depending on what type of enemy they are, there's also those boss Galagas, I think they're called.

00:11:46.900 --> 00:11:49.280
And they're the ones that go all the way to the top of the screen.

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:55.220
And they have these tractor beams that come down and they can take away your ship.

00:11:55.220 --> 00:11:57.320
And that actually does cost you a life.

00:11:57.420 --> 00:12:05.820
But instead, as a result, if you free that ship that was taken away, you can now have two ships fighting side by side.

00:12:06.040 --> 00:12:09.880
And in some ways, that is like the very first shmup power-up.

00:12:09.880 --> 00:12:21.940
Yeah. So when I was playing this game for the podcast, I completely forgot that this was an actual gameplay mechanic.

00:12:21.940 --> 00:12:40.920
And I just find it interesting because this is the only example that I can think of of a power-up being granted to you by failing first and then getting something back, like redeeming yourself later.

00:12:41.840 --> 00:12:49.140
Because, yeah, I hadn't played that way on the arcade or in the past times that I've played this game.

00:12:49.260 --> 00:12:58.700
But it ended up being super helpful to sacrifice the queen and then sort of get it back as extra firepower later.

00:12:58.900 --> 00:13:09.340
Yeah, it was cool. It actually had this like – I don't want to say realism about it, but there's an interesting like cause and effect to it that you never see with other power-ups that are just scattered out throughout.

00:13:09.340 --> 00:13:10.640
It's like, no, you lost that ship.

00:13:10.780 --> 00:13:16.400
You're a new ship. But now you can – if you save it, you can attach yourself to it and have extra firepower.

00:13:16.680 --> 00:13:21.340
Yeah, and programming-wise, it's actually not even that hard to figure out how they would do something like that.

00:13:21.460 --> 00:13:24.840
But like at that time, it just seems like something so complicated.

00:13:24.840 --> 00:13:30.340
You're like, wait, the life that I lost can now be brought back but as a power-up to my ship.

00:13:30.900 --> 00:13:33.300
And it's a really useful power-up, right?

00:13:33.300 --> 00:13:39.400
I'm sure once you guys figured out that you could recover it, you always started your game by getting caught.

00:13:39.680 --> 00:13:45.740
Yeah, and I don't see it so much as like a challenge in programming, something like that.

00:13:45.760 --> 00:13:51.560
It's more just the idea of coming up with that part of the game.

00:13:51.780 --> 00:13:58.000
Like I wouldn't think that they would go – I mean like not to pat them too much on the back,

00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:02.420
but this being 1981, that's pretty interesting.

00:14:03.040 --> 00:14:04.540
No, absolutely. And that's what I was saying.

00:14:04.860 --> 00:14:13.340
What I meant by programming was that to the player, it would seem like this impossible feat to like rescue your previous ship,

00:14:13.460 --> 00:14:15.960
like gain a life back in a way.

00:14:16.180 --> 00:14:19.580
But realistically, they weren't doing anything that intense.

00:14:19.780 --> 00:14:26.060
I've got to imagine in the arcade, this kind of became like one of those little legendary things

00:14:26.060 --> 00:14:29.020
where you see someone who's really good at the game have these two ships and kind of like,

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:30.360
oh my god, how did you get that?

00:14:31.380 --> 00:14:35.420
Like that kind of thing where it's like, oh, there's these crazy secrets in these games

00:14:35.420 --> 00:14:38.920
before like that was so almost taken for granted.

00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:44.880
And there's also a cool thing that's added to here that's a precision aspect of the game

00:14:44.880 --> 00:14:48.960
where it tells you – I believe it tells you after your game is over –

00:14:48.960 --> 00:14:54.920
the percentage that you hit the enemies versus missed.

00:14:54.920 --> 00:14:58.540
So it tells you your total shots fired and your total number of hits.

00:14:58.760 --> 00:15:01.400
And then you can get a hit slash miss ratio.

00:15:01.700 --> 00:15:03.300
Yeah, I was always pretty bad.

00:15:04.680 --> 00:15:08.940
It kind of knocks you down a notch because you think you did pretty well.

00:15:09.060 --> 00:15:10.920
And then you're like, oh, I only had like 56%.

00:15:12.940 --> 00:15:14.240
56 sounds pretty good.

00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:16.380
I mean, did you have a lower score?

00:15:17.400 --> 00:15:18.700
Yeah, I was doing pretty poor.

00:15:19.260 --> 00:15:24.400
I actually at first thought that there was just a problem with the precision of this game

00:15:24.400 --> 00:15:28.160
and like that my – you know, like the shoot that you do,

00:15:28.340 --> 00:15:32.680
like the bullet that you shoot is such a small little needle that I was like,

00:15:32.760 --> 00:15:36.880
oh, I'm just – like I need a bigger beam or something like that.

00:15:37.040 --> 00:15:40.220
But no, I guess I just suck at this game because I was averaging like –

00:15:40.220 --> 00:15:42.500
highest I ever got was maybe like 45.

00:15:42.680 --> 00:15:44.700
But a lot of times I would see like 36.

00:15:44.700 --> 00:15:46.880
Wow. I mean –

00:15:46.880 --> 00:15:48.620
Maybe I was just shooting a lot too though.

00:15:48.840 --> 00:15:51.700
If you do get – I mean, not only in your accuracy,

00:15:52.320 --> 00:15:55.740
do you get a hit to your ego at the end,

00:15:56.660 --> 00:16:01.920
but you're also punished for spamming because it does that thing

00:16:01.920 --> 00:16:05.400
where it only allows like one bullet to be on the screen at a time.

00:16:05.500 --> 00:16:07.140
So if you miss, it's going to be a while.

00:16:08.620 --> 00:16:11.900
Yeah, and the turbo controller works the same way too.

00:16:11.900 --> 00:16:17.100
So if you use the turbo controller, you can only shoot like two shots in rapid succession

00:16:17.100 --> 00:16:18.020
and then you have to wait.

00:16:18.820 --> 00:16:23.660
Yeah, I thought that that felt a little weird to me that it wasn't even necessarily just two shots for me.

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:26.200
It would be like sometimes there would be three in a row and then there would be one

00:16:26.200 --> 00:16:27.960
and then there would be four and then there would be two.

00:16:28.140 --> 00:16:33.660
It just felt like there was no – I'm hitting A the same speed every time

00:16:33.660 --> 00:16:38.360
and it was just – I felt like there was no consistency as to like when the gaps were in my firepower.

00:16:38.360 --> 00:16:41.380
I don't know if you guys felt the same way because I'm watching some videos of it now

00:16:41.380 --> 00:16:43.640
and it doesn't seem to be the same case.

00:16:43.860 --> 00:16:50.040
I don't know. I mean, when I was shooting – we'll get into the special stage, the challenge stage,

00:16:50.040 --> 00:16:58.100
but those stages are an example of like basically lining up the enemies for you

00:16:58.100 --> 00:17:00.400
so that you can get them all in rapid succession.

00:17:00.400 --> 00:17:07.380
And because they're all right in front of you because they're all like in a single file line

00:17:07.380 --> 00:17:12.000
right into your path of fire, you can shoot very rapidly.

00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:15.840
But if you miss, that's when you're getting those gaps in fire.

00:17:16.140 --> 00:17:17.060
Yeah, that's probably right.

00:17:17.340 --> 00:17:22.320
Yeah, so let's talk about that challenge stage because I actually found that to be like

00:17:22.320 --> 00:17:27.420
a fun version of the game that I almost wish was like a mode B kind of thing

00:17:27.420 --> 00:17:32.580
that you could just do on your own, like go through like no danger of getting blown up

00:17:32.580 --> 00:17:36.560
by any of the enemies or hurt, but just kind of like a target practice,

00:17:36.740 --> 00:17:38.020
learn the patterns kind of thing.

00:17:38.480 --> 00:17:40.320
Did you guys have as much fun with this as I did?

00:17:40.480 --> 00:17:46.740
Yeah, it always felt like a reprieve because again, like you said, you can't die in these.

00:17:47.600 --> 00:17:54.880
And it's very satisfying if you can get a full line of enemies that are going loop-de-loops

00:17:54.880 --> 00:17:56.340
before they can get away.

00:17:57.320 --> 00:18:01.280
Yeah, I personally, I kind of feel like I enjoyed this a little more than the regular stages,

00:18:01.280 --> 00:18:06.500
only because there's like an interesting level of like trying to time and strategize like

00:18:06.500 --> 00:18:12.180
where it almost feels a little bit like a phone, like a mobile game that you'd see now

00:18:12.180 --> 00:18:15.220
where it's like line up all these things at the right time and you'll get a bunch of points

00:18:15.220 --> 00:18:16.320
at once or whatever, you know.

00:18:17.320 --> 00:18:18.220
I can see that.

