Okay. Came out in 1988.

00:35:02.760 --> 00:35:08.120
Very much like Galaga, except for, and I've played this one too.

00:35:08.860 --> 00:35:16.120
I would say that if anybody's a fan of Galaga, this just seems like the best version of the Galaga line.

00:35:16.120 --> 00:35:19.220
Because it takes, it has power-ups.

00:35:19.480 --> 00:35:24.240
It has these all different types of enemies, like more than we usually have.

00:35:24.400 --> 00:35:30.800
And then they also have these bigger, fatter enemies that when you shoot them, fireworks come out.

00:35:30.940 --> 00:35:33.360
Like onto the screen and flash and they do this whole thing.

00:35:33.440 --> 00:35:36.080
And I'm like, wow, that is just visually breathtaking.

00:35:36.080 --> 00:35:49.540
Wow. So, so Mike, I have to ask, now that another one of your, another part of your rubric has been filled out, would you call this game a shmup?

00:35:49.700 --> 00:35:54.380
You know, this was the closest that it would have came for a while to being a shmup.

00:35:54.380 --> 00:36:05.000
But I still think it is just a fixed arcade shooter because you only deal with the enemies on, on hand for that specific stage.

00:36:05.000 --> 00:36:07.680
And then you just go to another wave of that.

00:36:07.920 --> 00:36:13.820
Okay. Then imagine taking a very long break for a while from Galaga.

00:36:14.460 --> 00:36:20.360
And finally, Galaxian 3, Project Dragoon comes out.

00:36:20.560 --> 00:36:21.360
That's insane.

00:36:21.680 --> 00:36:29.540
I love it. This game, I made a note, I made a note here on my playthrough of this because this was available for PlayStation only in Europe.

00:36:29.880 --> 00:36:39.620
So I imagine it's quite expensive to play this game, but it's an on-rails shooter that has like a first-person shooter look for the most part.

00:36:40.440 --> 00:36:53.060
But what's crazy about this is that it used, in the arcade, two different laser discs to simulate the background and foreground all at once.

00:36:53.220 --> 00:36:55.640
Wow. So you had all that going on.

00:36:55.720 --> 00:36:57.380
But then also, here's the craziest part.

00:36:57.380 --> 00:37:05.240
It was a special setup at the arcade for six players to play the same at once, to be like six different fighters.

00:37:05.240 --> 00:37:07.900
And you could shoot anything on this massive screen.

00:37:08.140 --> 00:37:12.500
So it was almost like a ride in an amusement park.

00:37:12.660 --> 00:37:18.400
Think of it like that. It was all, it was all, you know, like it had complete voice acting.

00:37:18.400 --> 00:37:20.640
You couldn't control where you were going.

00:37:20.840 --> 00:37:29.340
And you basically just sat in a chair and moved your, you know, your cannon wherever you wanted to point it at.

00:37:29.420 --> 00:37:32.160
And then you could shoot laser beams at the enemy Galaga.

00:37:32.340 --> 00:37:33.540
So when did this come out?

00:37:34.440 --> 00:37:36.720
This came out around 96.

00:37:37.340 --> 00:37:39.480
Okay. Was it successful?

00:37:40.460 --> 00:37:42.240
Oh, it officially came out.

00:37:42.320 --> 00:37:43.480
Oh my God. Nevermind.

00:37:44.220 --> 00:37:50.460
So that I'm thinking of the PlayStation port, but the actual game came out in 1990.

00:37:51.520 --> 00:37:53.060
Because that's super ambitious.

00:37:53.540 --> 00:37:55.160
That's super ambitious for an arcade game.

00:37:55.620 --> 00:37:58.960
Are you like looking at this stuff like almost first person wise?

00:37:59.160 --> 00:38:01.620
Or are you? Yeah, you're looking at everything first person.

00:38:01.620 --> 00:38:04.500
And it had like a, it was a very special setup.

00:38:05.300 --> 00:38:07.360
Here, I'll post it in our group chat right now.

00:38:07.520 --> 00:38:08.780
So this way you guys can see it.

00:38:08.780 --> 00:38:14.380
If you look at that, that link I sent, I'll link this in the show notes too.

00:38:14.780 --> 00:38:23.440
It looks like you're at the mall and then you have, you know, like six chairs waiting for anybody to sit down at.

00:38:23.660 --> 00:38:25.160
That's so, oh, okay.

00:38:25.240 --> 00:38:27.300
I see the, I see the schematics here.

00:38:28.040 --> 00:38:30.380
Right, right. It's pretty cool though, right?

00:38:30.480 --> 00:38:35.900
Like I love that idea of, you know, it doesn't really feel like a video game so much.

00:38:35.900 --> 00:38:40.780
But you do have like an overall health bar for your entire team.

00:38:40.780 --> 00:38:44.760
So you just have to make sure that like everyone's working all the time to shoot down all the enemies.

00:38:45.000 --> 00:38:51.860
But it seems like a really cool story that just takes place in this Galaga world where all the enemies are bugs.

00:38:52.600 --> 00:38:54.340
Yeah, I really like this.

00:38:54.720 --> 00:39:00.440
Like if you go into the Wikipedia page and, you know, hopefully people listening to this in the future can still see this.

00:39:00.440 --> 00:39:15.640
Because they have a super blurry cell phone image that would lead you to believe that this cabinet is some like secret Area 51 thing that they could only get a really quick look at.

00:39:17.180 --> 00:39:19.100
But it looks interesting.

00:39:20.100 --> 00:39:23.400
Yeah, I would sit in one of those.

00:39:23.400 --> 00:39:27.600
Yes, I think, Sean, you're not wrong in saying that it was super ambitious.

00:39:28.000 --> 00:39:32.340
And unfortunately, it's not the same thing on the PlayStation.

00:39:33.180 --> 00:39:39.300
Because you're not in a 360 degree room that like has other people playing with you on it.

00:39:40.860 --> 00:39:43.700
I thought they would have just sold that as like a peripheral.

00:39:43.940 --> 00:39:46.320
Like you buy the room that you play in.

00:39:47.200 --> 00:39:49.320
Yeah, they didn't think to do that.

00:39:49.620 --> 00:39:57.960
But I seriously would say that just watch a video of somebody playing this game because it was actually insane to play this.

00:39:58.080 --> 00:40:01.080
And I cannot believe that it exists on the PlayStation.

00:40:01.640 --> 00:40:02.520
It's a one of a kind.

00:40:02.520 --> 00:40:10.820
And after Galaxian 3, Project Dragoon, Attack of the Zolgir came out, which has no Galaga in the title.

00:40:11.020 --> 00:40:21.320
But it's just worth noting that it was like the sequel and used a lot of the same like it used the same devices and setups as Galaxian 3.

00:40:21.760 --> 00:40:27.160
So that's probably why they just did it to cut costs and say like, oh, we can repurpose those machines to keep going.

00:40:27.300 --> 00:40:29.480
You should only need to make all that hardware once.

00:40:30.400 --> 00:40:32.460
Right, right. Not like, oh, what do we do next?

00:40:32.580 --> 00:40:33.560
Oh, let's get 12 people.

00:40:35.660 --> 00:40:46.420
And the last thing I have on this list before it just gets into basically like remastered versions of Galaga is Galaga Destination Earth, which is on the PlayStation.

00:40:46.420 --> 00:40:57.560
And it's this weird mix of 2.5D horizontal stages, 2.5D vertical stages, and then goes full on 3D.

00:40:59.600 --> 00:41:06.820
Like first person look Star Fox, imagine like something like that, where it goes on rails for a minute.

00:41:06.820 --> 00:41:15.240
And the idea is that you have to go from each planet in our solar system to stop them from getting to Earth.

00:41:15.700 --> 00:41:23.000
Wow. And does each level have like a different, does it have a different background and all that?

00:41:23.000 --> 00:41:25.480
Yeah, everything is a different background.

00:41:26.040 --> 00:41:31.680
Everything is very, you know, it's like very poor polygons.

00:41:32.160 --> 00:41:34.540
They're not like, nothing looks great in this game.

00:41:34.900 --> 00:41:44.780
Yeah. But everything in the stage for the particular planet, whether you're going horizontal, vertical, or 3D, it all happens seamlessly.

00:41:44.780 --> 00:41:50.180
So you kind of just like, in the same level, you switch between those three different types of gameplay.

00:41:51.460 --> 00:41:53.260
Interesting. Yeah.

00:41:53.440 --> 00:41:58.260
I thought this one was cool, but it's too janky for me.

00:41:58.360 --> 00:42:01.300
You guys know how I feel about early PlayStation N64 games.

00:42:01.960 --> 00:42:07.340
We know. But this did remind me of a Space Invaders, since we were talking about that as well.

00:42:07.340 --> 00:42:15.500
There was a Space Invaders made for the PlayStation as well, which had a solar system theme going on of like the Space Invaders are actually coming to Earth.

00:42:15.620 --> 00:42:18.920
And you start on Pluto, which is no longer a planet.

00:42:19.380 --> 00:42:24.380
And you have to work your way to the Earth to like stop them.

00:42:24.520 --> 00:42:29.540
And that game was really good for a Space Invaders game and even had bosses and stuff.

00:42:29.720 --> 00:42:36.060
So if you're a fan of Space Invaders and you're listening to this Galaga episode, you owe it to yourself to play that Space Invaders game on the PlayStation 1.

00:42:36.060 --> 00:42:42.120
Well, I don't think anyone has anything else to say, so we'll move on to the Essential Games list.

