00:43:45.880 --> 00:43:52.260
And there are a lot of games that I could say that about and say that there's still something to go back and see.

00:43:52.460 --> 00:43:55.800
In this one, I just don't think there's that much to go back for in this one.

00:43:55.920 --> 00:44:01.920
There's too many other games you can play that have just vast improvements.

00:44:03.520 --> 00:44:05.880
Not that this game does anything particularly wrong.

00:44:05.880 --> 00:44:08.800
It's just to me, there's not enough to it.

00:44:09.380 --> 00:44:10.760
So I'm also going to say no.

00:44:11.120 --> 00:44:22.980
All right. For my take, I think in 1986, it was fine to preserve arcade games like this so they could be on the NES and you could experience them at home.

00:44:23.460 --> 00:44:31.780
But they appear to be so primitive next to our Essential Games list that it couldn't possibly deserve a spot on that.

00:44:31.780 --> 00:44:40.640
I think when you add that to the fact that it's already been preserved on consoles as early as 1983 on the Sega SG-1000.

00:44:40.640 --> 00:44:42.780
And that was like a perfectly fine port.

00:44:43.140 --> 00:44:54.060
What we really needed here was a Mode B or something that turned Galaga either into a shmup with recognizable Galaga-esque themes.

00:44:54.060 --> 00:44:58.340
